## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end security of hybrid AI platforms, spanning software supply chain and model governance, with practical incident response capabilities.

## Key Concepts
- Secure software supply chain and model governance
- SBOMs, image/model signing, and cryptographic attestations
- Provenance verification and reproducible builds
- Policy-as-Code enforcement with OPA Gatekeeper
- Runtime attestation in Kubernetes environments
- Data provenance tracking for training datasets
- Incident response playbooks and success metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: sign an image with cosign
const { execSync } = require('child_process');

function signContainerImage(imageUrl) {
  // Generate SBOM
  execSync(`syft ${imageUrl} -o cyclonedx-json > sbom.json`);

  // Sign the image
  execSync(`cosign sign --key cosign.key ${imageUrl}`);

  // Attach SBOM as attestation
  execSync(`cosign attest --predicate sbom.json --type cyclonedx ${imageUrl}`);

  console.log(`Successfully signed ${imageUrl}`);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assess practical handling of event-driven security, supply chain hygiene, and IR in a multi-tenant, cloud-native setup.

## Key Concepts
- Event signing and gateway controls for external inputs
- Least privilege IAM and tenant isolation
- Runtime policy enforcement (OPA)
- SBOM/SLSA for serverless artifacts
- IR playbook: containment, evidence, communication

## Code Example
```javascript
// Verify HMAC signature of incoming webhook events
const crypto = require('crypto');
function verifyEvent(req, body, secret) {
  const signature = req.headers['x-signature'];
  const expected = crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(body).digest('hex');
  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(signature), Buffer.from(expected));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle key rotation and incident response for webhook compromises?
- How would you test the event gateway's policy enforcement?
